of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-1,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-103668/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "744f58f2-59be-552a-bb2f-d0819f35c1ca"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103668"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-11883"] :question/problem "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $3{x}^{2}+6x+4y+15=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+6x+4y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+6x & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2x] & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3[(x+1)^{2}-1] & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2}-3 & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4y-15+3 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4y-12 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4(y+3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y+3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-1,-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-103679/selection/fc4c694d6c016efaa9e5cfb8689eb85b0ea02cd1224f188bd6d40312e0d1ed0c" :answer/id #uuid "c36e07be-10d5-5b65-acdb-1fb5ba5ff4aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103679/selection/c6a1f0b35fb86f12a0e1138b6e2c0461f265df1c7e058c326ad8966bfd682852" :answer/id #uuid "f52b007b-fd7f-5182-8210-717b301edba9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103679/selection/dba8047b9b21d0edd9e340c2c54e10a470ed337e9ade0ddb0627b6082199dce0" :answer/id #uuid "c142f931-6a6b-5e46-8757-5fbd90c94bee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-103679/selection/77