8x+6y+49 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+6y & =8x-49 \\\\ (y+3)^{2}-9 & =8x-49 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =8x-49+9 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =8x-40 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =8(x-5) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(2)(x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(5,-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-103675/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "393cdd18-ede6-5870-94ad-a093e67db2fd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103675"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7471"] :question/problem "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $5x^2 -10x - 4y - 7 = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-10x-4y-7 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x & =4y+7 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2x] & =4y+7 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =4y+7 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}-5 & =4y+7 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4y+7+5 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4y+12 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{5})(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k) = (1,-3).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5344"] :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $y$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8x-6y+33 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y & =8x-33 \\\\ (y-3)^{2}-9 & =8x-33 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8x-33+9 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8x-24 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4(2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k) = (3,3).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
